WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.696 --> 00:00:09.197
Welcome to Mount Rushmore!

2
00:00:09.597 --> 00:00:11.478
My name is George Washington.

3
00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:13.859
I'm Thomas Jefferson.

4
00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.501
My name is Abraham Lincoln.

5
00:00:17.461 --> 00:00:19.162
And I'm Theodore Roosevelt.

6
00:00:20.283 --> 00:00:22.264
We are the presidents of Mount Rushmore.

7
00:00:23.245 --> 00:00:28.008
Over fifty years ago, our faces were carved into these rocks here in the western state of South

8
00:00:28.068 --> 00:00:31.469
Dakota. You may be asking how the four of us got here.

9
00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:37.893
Well, that is a long story that began in December of 1923 with a man named Doane Robinson.

10
00:00:38.573 --> 00:00:41.455
Doane Robinson was a true lover of American history.

11
00:00:42.276 --> 00:00:46.918
His dream was to capture western American history in the rocks of South Dakota in a way that

12
00:00:46.958 --> 00:00:48.399
had never been done before.

13
00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:53.242
He wanted to create a memorial that would make South Dakota famous.

14
00:00:54.003 --> 00:01:00.137
You see, before our faces were put here, many people had never even heard of South Dakota, let

15
00:01:00.197 --> 00:01:01.577
alone come here for a vacation.

16
00:01:03.058 --> 00:01:08.539
But Robinson believed that people would love the mountains of South Dakota if there were something exciting to

17
00:01:08.579 --> 00:01:08.919
look at.

18
00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:13.521
Robinson talked to everyone he knew and told them about his great plan.

19
00:01:14.101 --> 00:01:18.982
Of course, some people laughed at him and said that his plan was foolish, but many others listened

20
00:01:19.002 --> 00:01:19.222
to him.

21
00:01:19.902 --> 00:01:24.964
They agreed that carving the faces of famous Americans would be a great historical monument for the United

22
00:01:25.024 --> 00:01:28.338
States and would bring many tourists to their small state.

23
00:01:29.559 --> 00:01:34.983
Within a few months, Robinson had convinced many important men, and they decided to start the project.

24
00:01:35.704 --> 00:01:38.606
Robinson found the only man in America that could do the job.

25
00:01:39.246 --> 00:01:40.928
His name was Gutzon Borglum.

26
00:01:41.588 --> 00:01:44.791
He was the most famous American artist and sculptor at that time.

27
00:01:45.831 --> 00:01:51.656
When Gutzon heard about this project, he was very excited and boarded the first train for South Dakota.

28
00:01:52.136 --> 00:01:58.461
Robinson was thinking about carving the faces of Western heroes, such as General George Armstrong Custer, Buffalo Bill

29
00:01:58.501 --> 00:02:00.082
Cody, or Lewis and Clark.

30
00:02:01.963 --> 00:02:07.947
However, when Gutzon Borglum arrived, he told Robinson that he wished to carve the faces of more remarkable

31
00:02:08.007 --> 00:02:10.709
Americans that would illustrate the history of our country.

32
00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:13.591
Naturally, he was thinking about the four of us.

33
00:02:15.352 --> 00:02:20.356
There are many mountains in South Dakota, but most of them were not suitable to carve our faces

34
00:02:20.396 --> 00:02:25.026
into. On some mountains, the rock was too soft or it had too many cracks in it.

35
00:02:25.626 --> 00:02:30.108
In other places, there was not enough sunlight, so our faces would always be in shadow.

36
00:02:31.628 --> 00:02:33.469
Gutzon searched for the perfect location.

37
00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:38.651
Then one day Gutzon came to a tall cliff that was surrounded by pine trees.

38
00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:43.773
The name of the mountain was Mount Rushmore, near the little town of Keystone.

39
00:02:44.474 --> 00:02:47.815
The rock was flat and hard, and it had the perfect amount of sunlight.

40
00:02:48.635 --> 00:02:49.736
"Here is the place!"

41
00:02:50.744 --> 00:02:51.464
Gutzon cried.

42
00:02:51.484 --> 00:02:54.305
"I will carve America's history here."

43
00:02:56.366 --> 00:03:01.427
It took Gutzon Borglum and 400 men almost 14 years to carve our faces into these rocks.

44
00:03:02.067 --> 00:03:07.108
The workmen were miners who had spent their lives smashing rocks, so at first many of them considered

45
00:03:07.148 --> 00:03:08.369
it to be a normal job.

46
00:03:10.549 --> 00:03:14.510
Since they were poor, they were happy to earn money to buy food for their families.

47
00:03:15.671 --> 00:03:20.012
But as our faces began to appear, they became much more excited about their work.

48
00:03:20.704 --> 00:03:24.666
They were not simply making money; they knew they were building American history.

49
00:03:25.966 --> 00:03:30.108
Norman Anderson was one of the carvers that helped make our faces look true to life.

50
00:03:30.648 --> 00:03:37.091
He was proud to say, "I put the curl in Lincoln's beard, the part in Teddy's hair, and

51
00:03:37.171 --> 00:03:38.932
the twinkle in Washington's eye."

52
00:03:40.192 --> 00:03:43.054
But how did these workmen make us look so lifelike?

53
00:03:43.854 --> 00:03:48.636
First, Gutzon made a statue of each of us, which he brought to the construction site.

54
00:03:49.308 --> 00:03:54.993
Then, using special measurement instruments, the workmen made an exact copy of the statues in the rock.

55
00:03:55.554 --> 00:03:59.858
The only difference was that the rock carving was 12 times bigger than the statues.

56
00:04:01.239 --> 00:04:05.723
A protractor was placed at the top of the statues' heads and a bigger one near the top

57
00:04:05.763 --> 00:04:06.304
of the mountain.

58
00:04:06.744 --> 00:04:11.588
To make Lincoln's eye, for example, the workers would use a string attached to the protractor on the

59
00:04:11.628 --> 00:04:15.292
statue to measure the distance from the top of his head to the top of his eye.

60
00:04:17.128 --> 00:04:21.914
Then they would use a string attached to the protractor on the mountain that was 12 times longer.

61
00:04:22.855 --> 00:04:28.281
Gutzon Borglum died just before our faces were completed, so he never got to see his final masterpiece.

62
00:04:29.002 --> 00:04:34.809
But his son, Lincoln Borglum, and the workmen spent one more year finishing this great piece of American

63
00:04:34.849 --> 00:04:37.552
history, which is now called Mount Rushmore.
